    "evidence_text": "Rapamycin passes the torch: a new generation of mTOR inhibitors\n\nIn recent years, interest has focused on its potential as an anticancer drug.\n\nWomen with LAM can also develop renal angiomyolipomas.\n\nThe regulation of miR-21 by Rapamycin is cell type independent.\n\nThe Drosophila DHR96 nuclear receptor binds cholesterol and regulates cholesterol homeostasis.\n\nMechanisms of Life Span Extension by Rapamycin in the Fruit Fly Drosophila melanogaster\n\nWe show here that feeding rapamycin to adult Drosophila produces the life span extension seen in some TOR mutants.\n\nIncrease in life span by rapamycin was associated with increased resistance to both starvation and paraquat.\n\nCOPI Complex Is a Regulator of Lipid Homeostasis\n\nReduced fat deposition in CR is linked to preservation of insulin responsiveness in older animals.\n\nDaily energy expenditure in MR animals significantly exceeds that of both PF and CF.\n\nDecreased proteasomal activity causes age-related phenotypes and promotes the development of metabolic abnormalities.\n\nTg mice exhibited a shortened life span and developed age-related phenotypes.\n\nDrosophila RNAi screen reveals CD36 family member required for mycobacterial infection.",
